@title(Lean Proofs, Checked in the Browser)

Write a proof in Lean 4 and IDoc checks it as you type. IDoc's own Lean front end builds each tactic's proof
term, and IDoc's proof kernel (Lean's logic, in WebAssembly) accepts or refuses it. Nothing is sent to a Lean
server. Open a theorem to see the goals before and after each step.

@section(Arithmetic: omega, simp, decide)

@proof(lean)
theorem add_zero' (n : Nat) : n + 0 = n := by
  simp

theorem linear (a b : Nat) (h : a < b) : a + 1 ≤ b := by
  omega

theorem small : 2 ^ 10 = 1024 := by
  decide
@end

@section(Induction)

A definition by equations, then the classic sums over it. @code{grind} is ring normalisation followed by
@code{omega}, and each step is still checked by the kernel.

@proof(lean)
def sumTo (f : Nat → Nat) : Nat → Nat
  | 0 => 0
  | n + 1 => sumTo f n + f n

theorem odd_sum (n : Nat) : sumTo (fun i => 2 * i + 1) n = n ^ 2 := by
  induction n with
  | zero => rfl
  | succ n ih => simp only [sumTo, ih]; grind

theorem gauss (n : Nat) : 2 * sumTo (fun i => i) (n + 1) = n * (n + 1) := by
  induction n with
  | zero => rfl
  | succ n ih => simp only [sumTo] at *; grind

theorem squares (n : Nat) : 6 * sumTo (fun i => i ^ 2) (n + 1) = n * (n + 1) * (2 * n + 1) := by
  induction n with
  | zero => rfl
  | succ n ih => simp only [sumTo] at *; grind

theorem powers_of_two (n : Nat) : sumTo (fun i => 2 ^ i) n + 1 = 2 ^ n := by
  induction n with
  | zero => rfl
  | succ n ih => simp only [sumTo, Nat.pow_succ]; omega
@end

@section(Inequalities)

@proof(lean)
theorem two_pow (n : Nat) : 2 ^ n ≥ n + 1 := by
  induction n with
  | zero => decide
  | succ n ih => rw [Nat.pow_succ]; omega

theorem bernoulli (x n : Nat) : (1 + x) ^ n ≥ 1 + n * x := by
  induction n with
  | zero => simp
  | succ n ih =>
    rw [Nat.pow_succ]
    have := Nat.mul_le_mul_right (1 + x) ih
    grind
@end

@section(A wrong proof is refused)

The first theorem is false, so no tactic can prove it, and the kernel says so. The second is true and is
accepted beside it.

@proof(lean)
theorem nope (n : Nat) : n + 1 = n := by
  omega

theorem fine (n : Nat) : n + 0 = n := by
  simp
@end

@section(Lean proofs as graded exercises)

The student writes tactics in the box and sees the goals and the verdict as they type. The mark comes from the
server, which runs the same front end and kernel on the text that was handed in. A proof that the student's own
browser claims is checked is still marked wrong if it is not.

@lab(prove, lean, id=lean1, pts=4, goal="(n : Nat) : 2 ^ n ≥ n + 1")
@end

@lab(prove, lean, id=lean2, pts=6, goal="(n : Nat) : 3 ^ n ≥ 2 * n + 1")
@end

@lab(prove, lean, id=lean3, pts=2, goal="(a b : Nat) (h : a < b) : a + 1 ≤ b")
@end
